One evening, she encountered the Ocean, who glowed with strange luminescence. "How do you shine without your own fire?" the Moon asked.

The Ocean laughed gently. "I do not create light—I reflect it. The Sun's rays reach even into my depths, and I return them as my own gift to the world. There is no shame in reflecting another's light; in doing so, I make it dance and shimmer in ways the Sun alone never could."

The Moon considered this wisdom. That night, she opened herself to the Sun's radiance, letting it wash across her surface. For the first time, she glowed with borrowed silver light, casting her beams across the sleeping world.

The Ocean recognized her glow and rejoiced, reflecting the Moon's reflection of the Sun—light shared, transformed, and shared again.

From that night forward, the Moon understood: true beauty lies not in burning bright alone, but in receiving light graciously and reflecting it back transformed. And so she waxes and wanes, forever dancing with the light that is both hers and not hers.
